    <string name="remosaic_steered">Directional interpolation</string>
    <string name="remosaic_steered_desc">Green is filled in along an edge, not across it. Averaging across an edge gives an in-between value, and the colour differences are computed from it: hence the colour fringes on letters and outlines</string>
    <string name="remosaic_clamp">Colour difference clamp</string>
    <string name="remosaic_clamp_desc">An interpolated difference stays within the ones measured nearby. On a flat surface it changes nothing; on an edge it cuts the overshoot, that very pink halo</string>
    <string name="remosaic_dump">Save the remosaic output</string>
    <string name="remosaic_dump_desc">Saves one more DNG next to the normal one: exactly what the node wrote, before demosaicing. It tells an error of the remosaic itself from an error of the later stages. Each shot takes 24 MB more</string>
    <string name="remosaic_flatfield">Block flat field</string>
    <string name="remosaic_flatfield_desc">Photosites inside a block differ in response, by up to 12 % on this sensor and differently for each colour. The profile is measured on the frame itself and divided out; otherwise it goes into the green field, and with the opposite sign into the colour differences: a colour cast on fine structure. The reference app does not do this, so it is off by default; turn it on as an experiment</string>
    <string name="remosaic_backend_title">Remosaic algorithm</string>
    <string-array name="remosaic_backend_entries"><item>SCAMERA</item><item>Tetra Detail v2 — after Vivo</item><item>SCAM Neural — NPU, root (experimental)</item><item>HP9 HexQuad — NPU, 6 frames (test)</item><item>Quad 2×2 — NPU, 4 frames (main, 2x ISZ)</item></string-array>
    <string-array name="remosaic_backend_values" translatable="false"><item>scamera</item><item>tetra_detail</item><item>scam_neural</item><item>hp9_hexquad</item><item>imx06c_quad</item></string-array>
    <string name="tetra_response_summary">Estimates the 4×4 pixel response and its smooth change across the frame. With too little data a common correction is used. If texture or colour changes, compare with the correction off.</string>
